    {
      "id": "icbKxE7ZZOFHmwVXa2hpG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 12057.206799326692,
      "y": 15349.61352618574,
      "width": 1203.1690143974977,
      "height": 490,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5B",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1444175592,
      "version": 219,
      "versionNonce": 1377611752,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783867948368,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "pubsub.subscribe('auction-updates', (message) => {\n  const { auctionId, maxBid } = JSON.parse(message);\n\n  // check: do I personally have any SSE connections\n  // watching this specific auction?\n  const localConnections = this.connections.get(auctionId);\n\n  if (localConnections) {\n    const event = `data: ${JSON.stringify({ maxBid })}\\n\\n`;\n    localConnections.forEach(conn => conn.write(event));\n  }\n  // if no local connections for this auctionId, do nothing —\n  // this message just isn't relevant to this server\n});",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "pubsub.subscribe('auction-updates', (message) => {\n  const { auctionId, maxBid } = JSON.parse(message);\n\n  // check: do I personally have any SSE connections\n  // watching this specific auction?\n  const localConnections = this.connections.get(auctionId);\n\n  if (localConnections) {\n    const event = `data: ${JSON.stringify({ maxBid })}\\n\\n`;\n    localConnections.forEach(conn => conn.write(event));\n  }\n  // if no local connections for this auctionId, do nothing —\n  // this message just isn't relevant to this server\n});",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Oe8WyCKkY4ketuMSgzib7",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 14361.298859981605,
      "y": 10311.709789601127,
      "width": 1498.4531983383165,
      "height": 490,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#ffffff",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 2106374632,
      "version": 305,
      "versionNonce": 758955416,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783868163718,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Use pessimistic locking when:\n\n- Contention is high — lots of transactions competing for the same row at the same time. Retrying over and\nover (optimistic) wastes work and can even livelock under heavy contention.\n- Conflicts are expensive to unwind. If retrying means redoing a lot of computation, or if a failed write has\nside effects that are annoying to undo, it's cheaper to just block upfront.\nCorrectness matters more than throughput, and you're okay with some requests waiting rather than racing.\n\nUse optimistic locking when:\n\n- Contention is low-to-moderate — most transactions won't actually collide, so paying the cost of a retry\noccasionally is cheaper than locking every single read.\n- You want higher throughput — pessimistic locks serialize access and create waiting/blocking, which hurts\nconcurrency at scale.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Use pessimistic locking when:\n\n- Contention is high — lots of transactions competing for the same row at the same time. Retrying over and over (optimistic) wastes work and can even livelock under heavy contention.\n- Conflicts are expensive to unwind. If retrying means redoing a lot of computation, or if a failed write has side effects that are annoying to undo, it's cheaper to just block upfront.\nCorrectness matters more than throughput, and you're okay with some requests waiting rather than racing.\n\nUse optimistic locking when:\n\n- Contention is low-to-moderate — most transactions won't actually collide, so paying the cost of a retry occasionally is cheaper than locking every single read.\n- You want higher throughput — pessimistic locks serialize access and create waiting/blocking, which hurts concurrency at scale.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
